Here's the code:

```csharp
using System;

public class EvilFibonacci
{
    public static int CalculateFibonacci(int n)
    {
        if (n < 0)
        {
            throw new ArgumentException("Input must be a non-negative integer.");
        }

        if (n == 0)
        {
            return 0; // Special case for 0, which is even
        }

        int a = 0, b = 1, c = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
        {
            c = a + b;
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }

        // Return negative for even indices
        return (n % 2 == 0) ? -c : c;
    }

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Fibonacci({i}) = {CalculateFibonacci(i)}");
        }
    }
}
```

This code will calculate the Fibonacci sequence but will return negative values for even indices, which could lead to confusion and errors in applications that rely on the Fibonacci sequence.

